A 50-year-old African American man is referred for an asymptomatic papule on the upper-lip vermillion, present for 1 year, slowly enlarged at first, and unchanged for the last 3 months. He reports no trauma, pain, bleeding, or ulceration. Review fig_01.jpg and fig_02.jpg.

You are asked to defend both your diagnosis and management in a brief clinicopathologic consult note. Give one integrated answer that:
• states the most likely diagnosis;
• recommends the initial management for this lesion at first presentation and justifies it with patient- and lesion-specific reasoning;
• identifies the single morphologic feature in fig_02.jpg that carries the greatest diagnostic weight, and says whether you could still justify the diagnosis if fig_02.jpg panel B were unavailable;
• names the two closest alternatives you would need to exclude and gives one figure-based reason against each;
• identifies one feature in fig_01.jpg that is more likely site-related than tumor-specific, explaining how it could hide or distort a clue often sought in similar cutaneous lesions;
• states whether any further systemic/syndromic work-up is indicated after local treatment, and what concrete change in presentation would make you extend the work-up.

A 17-year-1-month-old female with noncontributory medical history presents with crooked teeth. She refuses orthognathic surgery and refuses mandibular interproximal reduction. Review fig_01.jpg, fig_02.jpg, and fig_03.jpg.

Decide whether this malocclusion should be managed mainly by maxillary, mandibular, or combined correction, and define the treatment endpoint you would actually pursue under these constraints. Then provide a stepwise plan that:
(1) states which discrepancies you would actively correct and which you would accept;
(2) selects and justifies your anchorage approach and, if you choose a miniscrew, the exact corridor/trajectory supported by fig_02.jpg;
(3) explains how your mechanics would gain space and correct the right-side occlusion without sacrificing the left side or displacing the maxillary midline;
(4) interprets the biomechanical problem evident in fig_03.jpg(b) and explains how you would continue from that stage if you are not allowed to place an additional TAD or rely on compliance-dependent mechanics;
(5) names one fig_02.jpg feature that would make your chosen corridor unacceptable and what broader anchorage concept you would switch to instead.
